+import type { Finding } from "../finding.page-type.ts"
+
+export const everyPathAnswersASettledIndexForPagedPathsAndABaseIndexForTheRest = {
+  id: "01a07cd0-4409-7e34-8cd7-97fdb9a93a53",
+  pageTypeSlug: "finding",
+  slug: "every-path-answers-a-settled-index-for-paged-paths-and-a-base-index-for-the-rest",
+  domainSlug: "domain/akasha-check",
+  claim:
+    "`shadow.index.everyPath()` answers a mixed index while a change is judged. A path some page claims is already absent where the change takes that page away, and the files beside that page are absent with it. A path no page claims is answered whether the change takes that path away or not. No path the change adds is answered at all. Nothing says so on the shadow page or on the index page. A check working out what a change takes away from `everyPath()` alone therefore misses the page moves that check is built to catch, while catching the same move of a file no page claims.",
+  evidence:
+    "Measured on 2026-09-07 against the akasha root at 16ae78c67e9. A `Change` was built by hand over the root, taking one path away and adding that same path with `.moved.ts` on the end, reading every other path off disk. `shadowAt(root).index.everyPath()` was compared with `shadowAsked(change).index.everyPath()`, the second being what `checking.module.code.ts` hands a check.\n\nFor `checks/code-checks/pages/no-tmp/no-tmp.code-check.ts`, a page: base holds the path taken away, true; the change's shadow holds it, false. Base answered 122234 paths, the change's shadow 122229 — five fewer, the page and the four files beside it.\n\nFor `bunfig.toml`, `biome.json` and `tsconfig.base.json`, none of them claimed by a page: base holds the path taken away, true; the change's shadow holds it, true. Both answered 122240 paths in each of the three runs, so the count did not move at all.\n\nIn every one of the four runs the added path was answered by neither, so the settling adds nothing for a path the change writes anew.\n\nThe cause is in `settlingOver` in `pages/indexes/indexing/indexing.module.code.ts`. Its `paths` filing is built from `claimingIn`, which files the path a page sits at and the paths of the files beside that page. A path no page claims reaches that filing through nothing, so the path index keeps what the base commit had for it.\n\n`checks/code-checks/pages/typecheck/typecheck.code-check.code.ts:116` reads `index.everyPath()` and filters it by `change.after(one) !== null`. That filter reads as a guard against a base index, and it answers correctly under either reading, so it is no witness for which one holds.\n\nThe first design of `spelled-path-stays` derived the paths a change takes away as the paths in `everyPath()` whose `before` answered a body and whose `after` answered nothing. Under this measurement that derivation answers an empty set for a page move, which is the move the check exists for. The check as landed derives them from `change.changed` instead, and is right under either reading.",
+} as const satisfies Finding
